const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is special about Murchison Falls?', a: 'Murchison Falls is the point where the entire flow of the River Nile is forced through a gap just 7 metres wide before plunging 43 metres into the gorge below. By volume of water per metre of width, it is the most powerful waterfall in the world. The falls can be viewed from the top (after the boat safari hike), the base (by boat), or from the air (helicopter available).' },
  { q: 'How far is Murchison Falls from Kampala?', a: 'Murchison Falls is approximately 300km north of Kampala, taking about 5 hours by road via Masindi. A charter flight from Entebbe to Pakuba airstrip inside the park takes approximately 1 hour and is available for those wanting to save driving time.' },
  { q: 'Can I see rhinos in Murchison Falls?', a: 'Rhinos are not present inside Murchison Falls National Park itself. However, Ziwa Rhino Sanctuary is located on the main road between Kampala and Murchison and is a standard stop on most Murchison itineraries. Guided rhino tracking walks at Ziwa take 1 to 2 hours and cost USD 40 per person.' },
  { q: 'Is the Nile boat safari worth it?', a: 'The Nile boat safari at Murchison Falls is consistently rated as one of the best wildlife experiences in Uganda. The combination of massive hippo pods, enormous crocodiles, elephants drinking at the bank, prolific birdlife and the waterfall thundering above the boat at the end of the run is extraordinary. It is 3 hours well spent.' },
